Ladybird’s storage.sync keeps each extension’s items in a local file,
under the limits extensions expect: 102,400 bytes in all, 8,192 bytes
for any one item, and 512 items. Chromium enforces the same three
in its SettingsStorageQuotaEnforcer, and WebKit checks the per-item
limit in WebExtensionAPIStorageArea::set() and reports the same
quotaBytes() and quotaBytesPerItem(); Gecko’s ext-storage.js uses the
same 8,192-byte item limit.

Ladybird’s quota errors name the constant an extension sees, as in
“QUOTA_BYTES_PER_ITEM quota exceeded”. Chromium’s current enforcer puts
its own enum value’s name there instead (Resource:: kQuotaBytesPerItem),
which no extension can usefully compare against.
